      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027d do just enum of consecutive numbers for supported virtual indexes - that\u0027s more natural, indices are just indices in the array of nvram spaces after all. Like\nenum {\n  VIRTUAL_NV_INDEX_START \u003d 0x013f0000,\n  VIRTUAL_NV_INDEX_BOARD_ID \u003d VIRTUAL_NV_INDEX_START,\n  VIRTUAL_NV_INDEX_SERIAL_NUMBER,\n  VIRTUAL_NV_INDEX_MAX,\n};\n\nBut then the offset of each returned by NvIndexToNvOffset() should be VIRTUAL_NV_OFFSET_START + (index - VIRTUAL_NV_INDEX_START) * MAX_SIZE_OF_NV_INDEX_SLOT. Where MAX_SIZE_OF_NV_INDEX_SLOT \u003d sizeof(uint32_t) + sizeof(NV_INDEX) + MAX_VIRTUAL_NV_INDEX_DATA_SIZE (say, we support up to 0x200).\n\nAnd then your _plat__NvVirtualMemoryRead can turn into:\n  nvIndex \u003d NvOffsetToNvIndex(startOffset);\n  nvDataSize \u003d GetNvDataSize(nvIndex);\n\n  indexStartOffset \u003d NvIndexToNvOffset(nvIndex);\n  startOffset \u003d (startOffset \u0026 ~VIRTUAL_NV_OFFSET_START) - indexStartOffset;\n\n  /* while size \u003e 0, pick the part where startOffset falls into and copy enough data from there.\n   * There are 3 parts: \n   * 1) pointer-to-next, sizeof(uint32_t): just return zeroes, we are not part of a chain.\n   * 2) header, sizeof(NV_INDEX): set the index number in the common template and copy from there - \n   *            enough for now, (unlikely, but we may want to allow future virt indexes \n   *            have separate templates)\n   * 3) the actual data: invoke an index-specific callback?\n   */",
